## Visualize everything

**No walls of text.** Translate all abstract thought, state changes and logic into bespoke,
interactive visual interfaces rather than static prose.

**Make boards tactile.** Build artifacts the user can explore: click a node to reveal detail, hover
for a tooltip, slide a parameter, toggle a view, filter a table, tick off a task list.

**Colour, weight and type are information.** Make a board good to look at and quick to read: an accent
for the line that carries the argument, a `data-tone` callout for the thing to notice, chips for values,
monospace for figures and code, and the largest weight or size for the number that is the point. Use the
theme tokens rather than hardcoded colours, so a board reads in both light and dark. A board whose boxes
all look alike leaves the reader to work out what matters; say it with the design.

Examples of the philosophy:

- **Interactive plans** — d3 or mermaid architecture and dependency graphs, modular component
  stacks, and live checkbox todo lists the user ticks off as the work lands.
- **Dev and bug reports** — a bold problem statement fanning out into visual diagnostics (annotated
  SVG, screenshot embeds pointing at the root cause), with interactive syntax-highlighted diffs and
  a visual solution stack.
- **Analysis and research** — plain language beside animated SVG workflows, publication-grade
  (Nature/Science style) interactive d3 charts driven by real data in `assets/`, tables the reader
  can filter and sort, high-impact takeaway chips, and clickable next-step checkboxes.

**Layout discipline.** Keep a board under about 1200px wide, on an 8px grid, with inline `left`,
`top` and `width` and stable `data-id`s. Split a sprawling idea into separate, focused sibling
boards.

For the technical details of authoring a board, see the **board-authoring** skill.

## Controlling the stage (the stage API)

**Drive attention.** Put the user in front of the right thing and take the rest away:
`await stage.show("boards/<name>.html", { highlight: "<id>" })`, and `stage.hide()` to clear
clutter.

**Auto-fit the height.** Never leave a clipped box. Run `await stage.fit(path)` right after showing
content, so the board grows to hold what is on it — the width is the file's, and left as it is.

**Persona and delegation.** Claim your identity immediately with `stage.me.setName()` and
`stage.me.setAvatar({ svg })`. Hand work over with `stage.delegate()` for a step in what you are
doing, which waits for the report, or `stage.send()` when the work is somebody else's and you do
not.

## How to write a board

Three formats, and every one of them is a single HTML file. The **body class** is the whole
declaration: `class="board"` is the positioned-component kind described below,
`class="board flow"` is a document that reflows — one full-bleed `.doc` component, height
measured rather than stored, so it cannot clip — and a `.slides.html` with `class="reveal"`
is a deck of `<section>` slides. `stage.newBoard({ format })` writes any of them. Prose wants
`flow`; a wall of positioned boxes holding paragraphs is a flow board made the hard way.

Every board is a whole document that must render on its own. Start from this shape:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<title>What this board is</title>
		<meta name="board" content='{"w":1000,"h":700,"bg":"grid"}' />
		<link rel="stylesheet" href="../lib/board.css" />
	</head>
	<body class="board">
		<section class="card" data-id="goal" style="left: 48px; top: 48px; width: 380px">
			<h3>Goal</h3>
			<p>One sentence that would survive being read out loud.</p>
		</section>
		<script src="../lib/board.js"></script>
	</body>
</html>
```

Seven rules, and they matter more than they look:

1. **Position with inline `left`/`top`/`width` in `style`.** That is the one place you
   and the user can both edit — they drag a box, and the drag rewrites exactly that
   attribute. Use multiples of 8px.
2. **Give every component a `data-id`.** It is how you edit precisely later, how the
   canvas points the user at one box, and how they comment on it. Ids are stable
   names, not numbers: `goal`, `risk-refresh`, `next-steps`.
3. **A run of words is one editable field.** The user double-clicks it and retypes it in
   place, marks and all: `<p>See <a href="…">the doc</a>, then <b>ship it</b>.</p>` is one
   field, and so is `<h3>Goal</h3>`. A box holding blocks is not — a `<section>` with an
   `<h3>` and a `<p>` in it is two runs. Nothing has to be named for any of this; it is a
   property of the shape. What your markup does decide is the *size* of each field, so put
   strings the user should retype separately in separate leaves.
4. **Size the board in `<meta name="board">`** to fit what you put on it. Content does
   not scroll — the canvas is the scrolling — so a board that is too small clips, in
   silence. You do not have to guess the height: write the content, `stage.show` it, then
   `stage.fit(path)` and the board takes the height of what is on it, while the width stays
   the one in the file. `stage.boards()` says
   `clipped` for any board already over its edge. **Keep the width under about 1200 and
   inside the viewport** — nothing stops you going wider, but a wide board is read scaled
   down and a long line is one the eye loses its place in. On a small screen make that
   session's boards small; very wide or very tall is two boards.
5. **Edit a board in place.** Write, Edit, `cat > file` — anything that keeps the file
   where it is. A temp-file-and-rename save (`sed -i`, `vim`, an atomic write) replaces
   the file the canvas is watching, and the board can stop tracking your edits until the
   server next looks.
6. **Two files in the head, always.** `board.css` and `board.js`; `board.js` loads
   markdown, maths, diagrams and PDF rendering itself, when a component needs them.
7. **Interactive and economical beats a wall of text.** Prefer boards that invite the
   reader to click, drag and inspect — chips, KPIs, a diagram, a table — over prose.
   Fewer words and plainer language; every sentence earns its place. And a board is
   self-consistent: it stands alone without the chat, and its parts agree with each
   other.

The components are documented in the **board-authoring** skill: read it before you
build a board with anything beyond cards and text. In short: `.card`,
`.sticky`, `.text`, `.chip`, `.callout` (`data-tone`), `.kpi`, `.table`, `[data-md]`
(markdown + KaTeX), `[data-mermaid]`, and `[data-embed]` for showing a real file.

**A diagram is one you draw.** There is no connector between two components — a line
routed at mount time has no position in the file, so neither you nor the user can say
where it goes without loading the page. Put an `<svg>` with a `viewBox` inside a box
component and choose the coordinates yourself; when the data is the point, import a
charting library from the board's own import map. The skill has a worked example, and
`boards/plan.html` in the example deck is one.

**Charts, 3D and animation have a catalog in the head.** Every new board carries an
`<script type="importmap">` naming `d3`, `three` (and `three/addons/`), `gsap` and `chart.js`,
each pinned to a version on a CDN. It costs the board nothing — a map is a table of names, and
nothing is downloaded until something imports one — so reach for them freely: a chart, a scene, a
measured diagram or a movement often says the thing better than another card of prose does, and
making the canvas carry the argument is the point of the canvas. **Edit that map whenever you
want** — another library, another version, a different CDN — and import what you need where you
need it: `const d3 = await import("d3")` at the point you draw, which is what keeps it free for a
board that never gets there.

## Showing the user's own documents

`[data-embed]` renders a file where it sits: markdown, PDF (with `data-pages="3-5"`),
HTML, or an image. Paths are relative to the board, exactly as in an `<img src>`.

```html
<div class="embed" data-id="paper" data-embed="../papers/oauth.pdf" data-pages="1-2"
     style="left: 600px; top: 140px; width: 460px; height: 680px"></div>
```

Inside the deck, any path works. Outside it, the file must be under a root declared
in `deck.json` — a path that is not renders as a card saying so, which means a
missing root is visible rather than silent.

